WEBVTT

00:01.500 --> 00:04.279
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.347 --> 00:07.663
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:07.731 --> 00:11.131
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.215 --> 00:14.112
♪ On which we used to rely?

00:14.197 --> 00:17.748
♪ Lucky there's a family guy

00:17.799 --> 00:20.962
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.033 --> 00:22.571
♪ All the things that make us

00:22.639 --> 00:23.995
♪ Laugh and cry

00:24.080 --> 00:26.776
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.103 --> 00:36.476
(muffled):
Good evening, I'm Tom Tucker,

00:36.544 --> 00:38.585
and the reason I'm wearing
this mask is because

00:38.655 --> 00:41.064
we've got a Make-A-Wish
kid in the studio tonight,

00:41.132 --> 00:43.075
who, as you'll find out
as the show goes on,

00:43.143 --> 00:44.617
is quite a cougher.

00:44.684 --> 00:47.508
In local news, Mayor West
signed a bill today...

00:47.509 --> 00:48.063
(coughs)

00:48.067 --> 00:49.907
See? That's-that's
what I was talking about.

00:50.003 --> 00:51.860
(continues coughing)

00:53.309 --> 00:54.152
Are we gonna get that,

00:54.153 --> 00:56.011
or are we gonna wait
for commercial?

00:56.079 --> 00:58.223
No, no, you have
to spray it.

00:58.290 --> 00:59.930
You can't just wipe--
look, you know what?

00:59.998 --> 01:01.674
Let's just throw
the whole camera out.

01:01.743 --> 01:03.214
Okay, Joyce,
you talk now.

01:03.282 --> 01:04.448
I-I don't even want
to open my mouth.

01:04.449 --> 01:06.749
You know, Tom,
I haven't seen you in a mask

01:06.750 --> 01:09.161
since you went under the stage
name George P. Wilbur.

01:09.228 --> 01:11.070
Oh-ho-ho, Joyce,
you know we don't talk

01:11.138 --> 01:12.443
about that on the air.

01:12.511 --> 01:13.616
Oh, my God!

01:13.684 --> 01:15.123
Did she... did
she just say

01:15.193 --> 01:17.552
that George P. Wilbur was
Tom Tucker's stage name?

01:17.620 --> 01:19.596
Who the hell
is George P. Wilbur?

01:19.664 --> 01:22.344
<i>That's the guy who played
Michael Myers in Halloween 4,</i>

01:22.412 --> 01:24.505
the greatest movie
of all time.

01:24.573 --> 01:26.583
I've only seen that movie
about a thousand times.

01:26.634 --> 01:28.224
How could I not
have noticed that?

01:28.275 --> 01:29.730
(screaming)

01:29.781 --> 01:31.121
(crying)

01:31.206 --> 01:34.589
(continues screaming)

01:34.640 --> 01:36.984
(sobbing)

01:37.070 --> 01:38.476
TOM:
Good evening. I'm Michael Myers.

01:38.527 --> 01:40.049
I have enormous
psychological problems,

01:40.117 --> 01:42.966
and I'm going
to take them out on you.

01:43.033 --> 01:44.806
<i>It is him. I
can't believe it.</i>

01:44.874 --> 01:46.802
I mean, that's, like,
my favorite movie ever!

01:46.869 --> 01:48.787
<i>I thought The Flintstones
in Viva Rock Vegas</i>

01:48.788 --> 01:49.778
was your favorite movie.

01:49.783 --> 01:51.589
No, that's my
favorite period piece.

01:51.657 --> 01:53.469
<i>Well, that and Good
Night and Good Luck.</i>

01:53.536 --> 01:55.342
Even though I was tricked
into leaving early.

01:55.411 --> 01:57.420
Good night and good luck.

01:57.488 --> 02:00.301
And good night
and good luck to you, sir.

02:04.693 --> 02:06.248
Oh, hi, Chris.

02:06.316 --> 02:07.722
How was school?

02:07.790 --> 02:09.599
It was great.
I met a girl, Mom!

02:09.667 --> 02:11.290
That's wonderful, honey.

02:11.358 --> 02:13.384
Yeah, her name is
Lindsey, like the state.

02:13.452 --> 02:14.880
Is she nice?

02:14.905 --> 02:16.410
Yeah, she's really
nice and super pretty,

02:16.451 --> 02:18.277
and her bicycle seat
smells like strawberries.

02:18.344 --> 02:21.091
Oh. Okay.

02:21.158 --> 02:23.386
I'm gonna go upstairs
and alternate between

02:23.454 --> 02:26.784
hopeful excitement
and suicidal pessimism!

02:31.510 --> 02:33.386
(sighs)
Got to get the energy up.

02:36.936 --> 02:38.445
Found it.
(knocking)

02:38.512 --> 02:40.555
Hey, uh, excuse
me, Mr. Tucker.

02:40.622 --> 02:41.719
You got a second?

02:41.744 --> 02:43.352
Oh, hello, Peter.
Sure, come on in.

02:43.437 --> 02:44.817
Thanks. Um, hey, listen,

02:44.842 --> 02:46.348
I just found out that
you were the guy

02:46.385 --> 02:48.830
<i>who played Michael Myers
in Halloween 4,</i>

02:48.898 --> 02:49.866
and I just want to say,

02:49.891 --> 02:51.933
I think that was the most
brilliant performance ever

02:51.945 --> 02:53.285
in the history
of everything,

02:53.356 --> 02:55.830
and I wanted to see
if you would sign my DVD.

02:55.901 --> 02:57.874
Oh, I suppose.

02:57.925 --> 02:59.266
Thanks. Hey, so,
what happened?

02:59.334 --> 03:01.510
How come you never
did any more movies?

03:01.578 --> 03:03.455
Well, I tried to make a career
out there in Hollywood,

03:03.522 --> 03:06.101
but I just didn't realize
how tough acting is.

03:06.168 --> 03:07.307
Well, you know
what they say--

03:07.375 --> 03:08.813
"Tough actin' Tinactin."

03:08.882 --> 03:10.789
I wish I'd
known that then.

03:10.873 --> 03:12.395
<i>You see, life after
Halloween 4</i>

03:12.420 --> 03:13.846
wasn't exactly smooth sailing.

03:13.890 --> 03:15.815
Once I stepped out
from behind that mask,

03:15.899 --> 03:18.277
Hollywood gave me
the cold shoulder.

03:19.651 --> 03:21.963
<i>TOM:
The work I did manage to get</i>

03:22.014 --> 03:25.197
<i>was usually just bit parts
that didn't last too long.</i>

03:25.282 --> 03:27.859
<i>Like the role of Denise's
boyfriend on The Cosby Show.</i>

03:27.910 --> 03:29.350
It's nice
to meet you, Martin.

03:29.418 --> 03:30.791
Thank you,
Mrs. Huxtable.

03:30.859 --> 03:32.734
So, if you guys
are a family,

03:32.803 --> 03:35.014
why are none of you
the same shade of black?

03:35.082 --> 03:38.378
<i>I was also coked out
of my skull most of the time.</i>

03:38.430 --> 03:40.875
<i>But whatever the case,
I was done.</i>

03:42.449 --> 03:44.960
I left Hollywood, moved back
to my hometown of Quahog,

03:45.031 --> 03:47.289
and wound up working
here as a news anchor.

03:47.357 --> 03:49.769
Wow. Well,
don't you miss it?

03:49.838 --> 03:52.483
Every second of every day,
but I can't touch coke again.

03:52.554 --> 03:54.091
It would ruin my life.

03:54.159 --> 03:54.929
No, I mean acting.

03:54.996 --> 03:56.370
Oh. Of course.

03:56.438 --> 03:59.185
But it's over for me.
That's all in the past.

03:59.253 --> 04:02.131
Well, if you ask me, you got the
makings of a star.

04:02.200 --> 04:04.712
- And I want to help you get there.
- Well, that's a nice offer,

04:04.780 --> 04:06.990
but I'm not quite sure
how you expect to do that.

04:07.058 --> 04:09.268
Well, you've brought me
so much joy over the years

04:09.337 --> 04:10.976
as that lovable
murderer of teenagers,

04:11.045 --> 04:12.787
and I want to
return the favor.

04:12.855 --> 04:14.395
I don't even have
an agent anymore.

04:14.462 --> 04:16.807
Well, then how about this?
I'll be your agent.

04:16.876 --> 04:17.888
You?

04:17.913 --> 04:19.419
<i>Yeah, I've seen every
episode of Entourage.</i>

04:19.421 --> 04:20.994
All you need is
some fake hair

04:21.062 --> 04:22.905
and a bunch of Hobbits
hanging around you.

04:22.972 --> 04:24.629
You're on in five
minutes, Mr. Tucker.

04:24.697 --> 04:25.835
You don't belong here.

04:25.903 --> 04:27.208
You're better than this.

04:27.276 --> 04:29.120
<i>You're Halloween 4 better.</i>

04:29.187 --> 04:30.642
Look, I appreciate
your offer,

04:30.693 --> 04:32.735
but I've got a pretty
good thing going here.

04:32.803 --> 04:34.452
In a few minutes,
I've got to be on camera

04:34.478 --> 04:36.564
<i>in front of 800 people.</i>

04:41.564 --> 04:43.324
Good evening, Quahog.
I'm Tom Tucker.

04:43.391 --> 04:46.154
Our top story tonight: The
Rhode Island Historical Society

04:46.222 --> 04:49.237
is heralding the arrival
of a cast-iron, oak-handled

04:49.305 --> 04:50.979
Colonial bedsheet warmer,

04:51.047 --> 04:53.457
now on display
in the Statehouse Rotunda.

04:53.525 --> 04:55.100
<i>TOM:
Okay, there it is.</i>

04:55.168 --> 04:58.919
<i>As I understand it, they would
put the coal inside of that,</i>

04:58.987 --> 05:01.968
<i>and then put the object
between the sheets.</i>

05:02.036 --> 05:04.143
<i>It was very cold back then.</i>

05:04.211 --> 05:06.121
<i>And it would warm the sheets.</i>

05:07.965 --> 05:10.008
<i>And I-I think that's
the gist of it.</i>

05:10.076 --> 05:11.918
<i>How much footage
of this do we have?</i>

05:11.986 --> 05:13.660
<i>Oh-oh, this is live.</i>

05:13.728 --> 05:15.066
<i>This... Can-can she hear me?</i>

05:15.133 --> 05:17.411
<i>Okay, you can put it down now.</i>

05:17.479 --> 05:20.024
<i>You... She can't hear me. Okay.</i>

05:21.533 --> 05:23.609
<i>What the hell is going on here?</i>

05:23.677 --> 05:26.156
<i>Is it... is it...
Am I having a stroke?</i>

05:28.302 --> 05:29.807
Wait, Peter! Don't go!

05:29.875 --> 05:31.667
So, you changed
your mind, huh, buddy?

05:31.736 --> 05:33.326
We going to Hollywood?

05:33.378 --> 05:35.083
You betcha.
But I have to say,

05:35.152 --> 05:37.008
I thought you'd
drive a fancier car.

05:37.036 --> 05:38.164
What? Why?

05:38.165 --> 05:40.814
Well, aren't you the Peter Griffin
of Peter Griffin Cadillac?

05:40.883 --> 05:42.121
No, that's another guy.

05:42.189 --> 05:44.128
Hi. I'm Peter Griffin,

05:44.197 --> 05:47.162
and at Peter Griffin Cadillac,
we believe in three things:

05:47.230 --> 05:49.975
fair dealing, family,
and friendship.

05:50.044 --> 05:51.885
I've lived in Quahog
my whole life.

05:51.952 --> 05:53.357
I love it here.

05:53.427 --> 05:55.738
And you're gonna love
a Peter Griffin Cadillac.

05:55.806 --> 05:56.843
Scumbag.

06:03.582 --> 06:05.041
All right, Lois,
remember to feed the cat,

06:05.066 --> 06:06.572
and remember to get a cat.

06:06.646 --> 06:08.656
I still don't understand
why you got to go

06:08.724 --> 06:10.164
all the way
to Hollywood.

06:10.231 --> 06:12.408
Because I know Tom Tucker
could be a great actor,

06:12.476 --> 06:14.551
and I'm gonna help him
make it happen.

06:14.618 --> 06:16.497
You see, everybody
needs just one person

06:16.565 --> 06:18.002
to believe in
their dreams

06:18.070 --> 06:20.548
so they can spread
their wings and fly.

06:20.616 --> 06:22.224
Dad, can I have money
for dance classes?

06:22.291 --> 06:23.799
Sit down, you
dumpy bread loaf.

06:23.867 --> 06:25.440
<i>I'd pay to see
you not dance.</i>

06:25.509 --> 06:27.215
- When will you be back?
- I don't know.

06:27.283 --> 06:28.891
But I'll be sure
to be back

06:28.959 --> 06:30.466
before Chris's big
dance class recital.

06:30.534 --> 06:32.039
I believe
in that boy.

06:32.107 --> 06:33.446
I don't want
to dance!

06:33.515 --> 06:34.654
You'll dance!

06:42.729 --> 06:44.202
Boy, I don't
know, Peter,

06:44.253 --> 06:46.298
being back out here makes me
kind of nervous.

06:46.382 --> 06:47.720
Maybe-maybe this
was all a mistake.

06:47.771 --> 06:49.444
Don't be silly, Tom--
you're an actor,

06:49.528 --> 06:51.152
and this is where you belong.
Now, let's take some of

06:51.236 --> 06:54.051
this complimentary luggage
and get going.

06:55.793 --> 06:57.937
Well, so, h-how
do we do this?

06:57.989 --> 06:58.758
You do nothing.

06:58.783 --> 07:00.291
You just sit back
and let your agent

07:00.836 --> 07:02.512
knock on every door in
Hollywood till he gets

07:02.537 --> 07:03.742
you an acting job.

07:04.136 --> 07:06.648
And that starts right now.

07:06.700 --> 07:08.389
Yes? C-Can
I help you?

07:08.457 --> 07:09.864
No, but I can help you.

07:09.932 --> 07:11.574
You need to hire this man.

07:11.641 --> 07:14.086
Oh. Um, okay.

07:14.154 --> 07:17.435
Well, do you have any experience
handling bags?

07:17.503 --> 07:18.908
Aha! I see!

07:18.976 --> 07:20.652
Typical casting-couch stuff.

07:20.721 --> 07:22.761
All right, kid,
you want to make it

07:22.832 --> 07:24.221
in showbiz
or don't you?

07:29.129 --> 07:31.674
I thought the airport
was Hollywood.

07:36.768 --> 07:40.316
Hey, Mom, I want you to
meet my new girlfriend.

07:40.384 --> 07:41.422
New girlfriend?

07:41.490 --> 07:42.829
Yeah, you remember that girl

07:42.897 --> 07:44.304
Lindsey I told you about?

07:44.372 --> 07:47.754
Well, I asked her out,
and now we are dating.

07:47.821 --> 07:50.853
Lindsey, this is my mom,
my little brother Stewie,

07:50.922 --> 07:52.730
and my dog Brian.

07:52.797 --> 07:53.600
Hey.

07:53.669 --> 07:55.980
Oh, my God.

07:56.048 --> 07:57.487
My goodness,
look at you.

07:57.554 --> 08:00.805
Do you even realize
who you look like?

08:00.873 --> 08:02.010
Cameron Diaz,
that's who.

08:02.078 --> 08:04.556
You are gorgeous!

08:04.624 --> 08:06.433
<i>Well, she's awful pretty-- I
know that, Mom.</i>

08:06.500 --> 08:07.739
Are you kidding me?

08:07.806 --> 08:09.079
Are they not seeing this?

08:09.146 --> 08:10.787
I know, it's kind of creepy.
It's almost like

08:10.856 --> 08:12.797
he's dating Lois.
Yeah.

08:12.865 --> 08:14.875
Looks like somebody's
getting a little Oedipus-y.

08:14.959 --> 08:16.800
- Can we say that?
- Just did.

08:20.554 --> 08:22.430
All right, Tom, this is
totally gonna work.

08:22.498 --> 08:24.255
All the biggest Hollywood
players eat here,

08:24.322 --> 08:26.885
and we just got to make them
think we're one of them.

08:26.953 --> 08:29.263
That's why I got these
old Hollywood Jew glasses.

08:29.314 --> 08:30.570
Waiter! Oh, waiter!

08:30.621 --> 08:32.598
Yes sir. Are you gentlemen
ready to order?

08:32.666 --> 08:35.728
Yes, I will have the Big and Fancy,
and my client here

08:35.795 --> 08:36.815
will have a bottle
of your best steak.

08:36.840 --> 08:38.044
Right away, sir.

08:38.229 --> 08:39.557
Peter, I-I'm not sure
what any of this

08:39.558 --> 08:41.097
has to do with
getting me acting jobs.

08:41.122 --> 08:42.528
Tom, who's the agent here?

08:42.574 --> 08:44.114
- You are.
- And who is the actor?

08:44.181 --> 08:45.925
Well, I am.

08:45.993 --> 08:48.134
Wait, which one am I again?
The agent.

08:48.185 --> 08:50.663
And who's paying for dinner?
I assumed you were.

08:50.730 --> 08:53.042
Ah, then we best load
our pockets with bread

08:53.109 --> 08:55.638
and get the hell
out of here.

08:55.723 --> 08:56.796
We have to go!

08:56.863 --> 08:59.457
Not expensive enough!

08:59.525 --> 09:02.053
Excuse me, I couldn't help but
notice your glasses,

09:02.121 --> 09:05.271
your poor table manners and
your huge sense of entitlement.

09:05.339 --> 09:06.711
- Are you an agent?
- I am.

09:06.779 --> 09:09.191
You've got ten seconds
to dazzle me. Go.

09:09.259 --> 09:11.921
<i>Well, I'm the casting
director for NCIS,</i>

09:11.988 --> 09:14.667
and we need an actor for a
mall role that shoots tomorrow.

09:14.734 --> 09:16.477
This is your man.

09:16.545 --> 09:18.623
That's great! He's got just
the look we're after.

09:18.691 --> 09:21.066
Show up here at
10:00 a.m. tomorrow.

09:21.136 --> 09:22.492
How about that, huh, Tom?

09:22.560 --> 09:23.798
You're back in
the business.

09:23.866 --> 09:25.673
Wow, Peter,
I can't believe it!

09:25.740 --> 09:27.063
Hey, don't be
so surprised.

09:27.131 --> 09:29.073
I've been pounding the
pavement for you, buddy.

09:29.142 --> 09:31.084
I even gave out all
my business cards.

09:31.151 --> 09:33.865
Call me! Call me! Call me!

09:33.917 --> 09:36.478
Call me! Call me!
Call me! Call me!

09:36.546 --> 09:39.310
Ah, show business.

09:42.525 --> 09:44.970
Oh, hi, you two.

09:45.038 --> 09:47.652
You know, Lindsey, I just
want to say how nice it is

09:47.720 --> 09:49.861
to have such a beautiful young
woman in our home.

09:49.929 --> 09:51.101
Oh, a baby.

09:51.169 --> 09:52.844
I almost had one
of you twice.

09:52.928 --> 09:55.373
(laughs)
Who didn't, right?

09:55.441 --> 09:57.787
So, what are you
two up to tonight?

09:57.854 --> 09:59.611
Well, we really
haven't decided yet.

09:59.679 --> 10:01.571
Eh, I'll think of something
while I'm taking

10:01.639 --> 10:03.614
a chick dump in your
parents' bathroom.

10:03.682 --> 10:06.194
And I will
hold your purse.

10:06.261 --> 10:07.532
Good for Chris, huh?

10:07.601 --> 10:09.244
He picked a
peach, didn't he?

10:09.312 --> 10:12.326
Lois, don't you think maybe part
of the reason you like Lindsey

10:12.394 --> 10:14.235
could be because she
looks so much like you?

10:14.302 --> 10:15.425
(chuckles): What?

10:15.493 --> 10:17.116
Come on, she's clearly
an awful person.

10:17.184 --> 10:18.957
And I think it's possible
that your vanity

10:19.026 --> 10:20.818
has made you
a little blind to that.

10:20.886 --> 10:23.733
Oh... I see what's
going on here-- you're jealous.

10:23.801 --> 10:26.212
Chris has a wonderful
young woman in his life,

10:26.280 --> 10:27.785
and you've got nothing.

10:27.856 --> 10:28.991
She's kind of
right, Brian.

10:29.058 --> 10:31.135
All you've got is
a worn-out rope toy

10:31.203 --> 10:34.320
and that squirrel who comes
by once a day to taunt you.

10:34.388 --> 10:36.514
(rustling in brush)
Hey!

10:36.581 --> 10:38.691
Hey, get out of here!
This is our yard!

10:38.759 --> 10:40.501
You don't belong there!
Don't you touch anything!

10:40.569 --> 10:41.807
I told you all this yesterday!

10:53.249 --> 10:56.398
To your first big
TV acting job.

10:56.465 --> 10:58.474
<i>MALE TV ANNOUNCER:
We now return to NCIS.</i>

10:58.543 --> 11:00.318
Oh, here it is.
Here comes my part.

11:01.557 --> 11:02.997
Excuse me.

11:03.064 --> 11:04.841
I'm looking for
Ed Manzelli.

11:04.909 --> 11:06.482
Good evening.
I'm Ed Manzelli.

11:06.550 --> 11:08.157
What can I do for you?

11:08.226 --> 11:10.400
I'm looking for a guy
who used to work here.

11:10.468 --> 11:12.412
Name's Mario.
Got any idea where he could be?

11:12.480 --> 11:14.320
I haven't seen him
in over a month.

11:14.388 --> 11:15.962
That's the end of my line.

11:16.029 --> 11:18.006
<i>Your line, NCIS.</i>

11:18.073 --> 11:20.955
You son of a bitch,
you made me feel again.

11:21.040 --> 11:23.501
Oh, don't you go
'cause I'll go.

11:23.569 --> 11:26.030
You know, Peter, I
just want to thank you

11:26.098 --> 11:27.538
for all you've
done for me.

11:27.606 --> 11:29.379
You... You are one
hell of an agent.

11:29.447 --> 11:31.323
Well, with you
as a client, it's easy.

11:31.391 --> 11:32.480
WOMAN (over intercom):
Mr. Griffin?

11:32.548 --> 11:33.903
Yes, Sandra?
You have a secretary?

11:33.970 --> 11:35.812
She calls herself
an assistant, but yes.

11:35.882 --> 11:37.121
What is it, Sandra?

11:37.189 --> 11:38.795
There's a man
here to see you.

11:38.863 --> 11:41.608
He says he wants to talk
to you about being his agent.

11:41.676 --> 11:43.719
Really?! Well, then,
by all means, send him in!

11:43.787 --> 11:45.311
Hey, knucklebutts!

11:45.379 --> 11:47.589
BOTH (gasp):
James Woods?!

11:47.658 --> 11:49.229
But I... I don't understand!

11:49.298 --> 11:50.974
I... I thought you were dead!

11:51.042 --> 11:52.546
Well... (chuckles)
I'm not.

11:52.614 --> 11:53.753
I don't believe you.

11:53.821 --> 11:55.294
God, is that true?

11:55.362 --> 11:56.701
God?

11:56.768 --> 11:59.313
God, are you there?

11:59.382 --> 12:02.095
(music playing
through headphones)

12:05.430 --> 12:08.376
Come on, don't touch yourself.
You're God.

12:15.087 --> 12:17.162
I-I don't get it.
Last time we saw you,

12:17.230 --> 12:20.011
Diane Simmons had just stabbed
you in the back with a knife.

12:20.078 --> 12:22.286
Yeah! How the hell
did you come back to life?!

12:22.354 --> 12:23.748
Well, gentlemen,

12:23.832 --> 12:26.543
sometimes being a big Hollywood
star has its advantages.

12:31.569 --> 12:33.727
Hey, is this guy somebody?

12:33.798 --> 12:35.472
Yeah, that's James Woods!

12:35.540 --> 12:36.878
We got a celebrity here!

12:36.946 --> 12:38.974
I repeat, this is not
a normal dead person!

12:39.042 --> 12:40.079
This is a celebrity!

12:42.290 --> 12:44.215
<i>JAMES: Being a famous movie star
entitled me</i>

12:44.283 --> 12:45.973
<i>to topnotch medical care</i>

12:46.025 --> 12:49.409
<i>not available
to the rest of society.</i>

12:49.493 --> 12:52.037
<i>My body was immediately taken
to a Hollywood hospital,</i>

12:52.088 --> 12:55.757
<i>where I was hooked up
to a 17-year-old ingenue.</i>

12:58.204 --> 13:00.298
<i>And in accordance
with Hollywood law,</i>

13:00.383 --> 13:02.089
<i>her life force was infused
into me,</i>

13:02.157 --> 13:04.785
<i>bringing me back from the dead.</i>

13:04.870 --> 13:06.712
<i>TOM:
What happened to the girl?</i>

13:06.780 --> 13:08.706
<i>JAMES: I threw her out a car window
at Nora Ephron.</i>

13:10.968 --> 13:12.574
Stop making
Jack Nicholson a pansy!

13:12.641 --> 13:14.486
You're thinking of Nancy Meyers!

13:14.553 --> 13:16.494
You're the same thing!

13:16.564 --> 13:17.970
Wow. That's amazing.

13:18.037 --> 13:20.349
So, what do you say, Peter?
Will you be my agent?

13:20.416 --> 13:21.672
I don't get it.
Why would a big shot

13:21.739 --> 13:23.111
like you want me for an agent?

13:23.179 --> 13:25.170
Because you're an up-and-comer,
because you're hungry.

13:25.222 --> 13:26.427
I am hungry.

13:26.511 --> 13:28.071
But you put me
and my family

13:28.138 --> 13:29.945
through a lot of
crap over the years.

13:30.014 --> 13:31.519
Why would I want
to help you?

13:31.587 --> 13:32.759
Well, because
you're my agent.

13:32.827 --> 13:34.302
Oh, my God, you're
right! I am!

13:34.370 --> 13:35.608
Hey, hold on now.

13:35.693 --> 13:37.466
<i>You came out here
to be my agent.</i>

13:37.534 --> 13:39.311
Tom, relax,
I can take care of you both.

13:39.379 --> 13:41.052
You'd be surprised
how much I can handle.

13:41.120 --> 13:43.933
Peter, I need you to drop
Stewie off at day care,

13:44.001 --> 13:46.445
and then shred this big
stack of old bank statements.

13:46.496 --> 13:48.055
You got it, Lois.

13:50.265 --> 13:52.107
Is he always
this quiet?

13:55.324 --> 13:58.239
So, Kevin made another attempt
on his life last night.

13:58.307 --> 14:00.316
He drank two bottles
of dish soap.

14:00.383 --> 14:01.925
Well, we have
some news, too.

14:01.992 --> 14:04.605
Chris is dating the
most wonderful girl.

14:04.673 --> 14:07.217
You know, everyone said, "Don't
smoke during your pregnancy,"

14:07.284 --> 14:10.500
but I think some of it rubbed
off on him 'cause now he's cool.

14:10.567 --> 14:11.939
That's nice.

14:12.010 --> 14:13.683
Well, I should go, Lois.

14:13.751 --> 14:16.480
Yeah, I should probably
get going myself.

14:16.547 --> 14:17.854
Oh, my God!

14:20.872 --> 14:22.359
What? What is it, Lois?

14:22.427 --> 14:23.734
That girl over there--

14:23.802 --> 14:25.709
that... that's
Chris's girlfriend!

14:25.777 --> 14:28.490
Hey, you know, she kind of
looks like you, Lois.

14:28.559 --> 14:30.766
<i>Maybe she'll thank me
for buying the coffee.</i>

14:30.835 --> 14:32.912
You think she
looks like me, too?

14:32.981 --> 14:34.723
Oh, my God.

14:34.790 --> 14:36.632
I'm starting to
think Brian was right.

14:36.699 --> 14:39.848
Maybe I have been blinded
by my own stupid vanity.

14:39.916 --> 14:41.623
That girl is
nothing but trouble.

14:41.691 --> 14:43.501
Are you gonna tell Chris?

14:43.568 --> 14:45.242
I guess I'll have to,

14:45.310 --> 14:47.520
but he doesn't take
bad news very well.

14:47.587 --> 14:50.769
Oh, Chris, honey,
I'm so sorry to tell you this,

14:50.837 --> 14:53.148
but your grandfather died.

14:53.216 --> 14:54.387
(gasping and hissing)

14:54.455 --> 14:55.728
(screaming)

14:55.795 --> 14:58.307
(yelling)

15:01.860 --> 15:03.032
All right, gentlemen.

15:03.099 --> 15:04.941
So, what do you got
for my client?

15:05.009 --> 15:06.749
Well, we're making a
mediocre action film,

15:06.817 --> 15:09.463
and we think James is just the
guy to do a serviceable job

15:09.531 --> 15:12.210
in a supporting role that
Jeremy Irons, Jeff Goldblum,

15:12.278 --> 15:14.322
and Craig T. Nelson
have already passed on.

15:14.390 --> 15:16.634
Well, we'd need
to see a script first.

15:16.702 --> 15:17.840
Oh, looks good.

15:17.908 --> 15:19.180
What do you think, James?

15:19.248 --> 15:20.285
I like it.
Good.

15:20.353 --> 15:21.859
I say we move on this.

15:21.926 --> 15:23.233
All right, then,
let's negotiate.

15:23.301 --> 15:24.890
- We want everything.
- We'll give you nothing.

15:24.958 --> 15:26.617
- We want something.
- Deal.

15:27.674 --> 15:29.265
(phone ringing)

15:31.309 --> 15:33.349
Ugh, not this hillbilly
from Rhode Island again.

15:33.418 --> 15:35.596
No thank you.
(beep)

15:35.664 --> 15:37.135
This is Peter Griffin,
famous agent,

15:37.205 --> 15:38.846
but you can call me PG-13.

15:38.914 --> 15:40.119
Ba-boom!

15:40.187 --> 15:41.392
Leave a message.
(beep)

15:41.460 --> 15:42.765
Hi, Peter, it's Tom.

15:42.833 --> 15:44.608
Uh, just trying
to reach you again.

15:44.675 --> 15:46.214
And you know,
about that meeting

15:46.282 --> 15:48.125
you set up with me
and Heath Ledger...

15:48.193 --> 15:49.767
He... He never showed.

15:49.835 --> 15:52.380
Maybe I was at the wrong
Panda Express, but I...

15:52.447 --> 15:54.724
Anyway, uh, call me back.

15:57.673 --> 15:59.901
Hey, listen, thanks for
inviting me to lunch, Peter.

15:59.968 --> 16:01.341
I-I was starting
to get nervous

16:01.409 --> 16:02.950
when you weren't
returning my calls.

16:03.018 --> 16:05.630
Tom, I... I didn't know
how to say this to you,

16:05.699 --> 16:07.406
so, here,
take this piece of paper.

16:07.473 --> 16:11.291
And you're on in four,
three, two...

16:11.359 --> 16:13.737
"Peter Griffin drops Tom Tucker
as a client."

16:13.806 --> 16:16.184
You're-you're dropping me
as a client?

16:16.251 --> 16:17.523
Yeah, just heard it
on the news.

16:17.591 --> 16:18.813
How could you do this?!

16:18.881 --> 16:20.286
Look, it's a whole
different landscape

16:20.354 --> 16:21.492
out here since Thursday.

16:21.559 --> 16:23.036
Everything's on computers now.

16:23.104 --> 16:25.245
Peter, I left a very
good job for this!

16:25.313 --> 16:27.088
You're the only reason
I came back out here!

16:27.156 --> 16:28.495
I wish I could help you, Tom,

16:28.563 --> 16:29.769
but this is coming
from upstairs.

16:29.838 --> 16:31.108
The karate studio?

16:31.176 --> 16:32.616
Tom, I have to go now.

16:32.684 --> 16:36.033
But if you're ever Kevin James,
I want you to give me a call.

16:39.232 --> 16:41.814
(phone ringing and buzzing)

16:41.881 --> 16:43.286
Mm. Hello?

16:43.354 --> 16:45.094
Peter, it's me, Woodsy!
I need a favor.

16:45.163 --> 16:47.641
What?! It's, like, 2:00 a.m.

16:47.710 --> 16:49.418
Yeah, so listen, I'm
at Chateau Marmont,

16:49.486 --> 16:51.059
I just met this
18-year-old chick,

16:51.127 --> 16:53.338
and I can't decide
whether to take her home

16:53.406 --> 16:55.113
or keep doing blow
with Tom Sizemore

16:55.180 --> 16:57.675
and see if I can do
better later in the night.

16:57.744 --> 16:58.915
Well, okay, um...

16:58.984 --> 17:00.521
Do, uh, do-do you love her?

17:00.589 --> 17:02.700
What?! Look, I'm
texting you her picture.

17:02.768 --> 17:05.482
Just tell me if you
think she's hot enough.

17:05.549 --> 17:06.887
(phone chirps)

17:06.958 --> 17:08.948
I... What is this
a picture of?

17:09.016 --> 17:11.127
Is that the bottom
of a white pumpkin?

17:11.196 --> 17:12.701
Is she hot or not?

17:12.769 --> 17:14.376
I-I guess.
Okay, good, great.

17:14.443 --> 17:17.141
So clear out of your apartment;
we're gonna be there in 15.

17:17.209 --> 17:18.398
What?! I'm sleeping.

17:18.465 --> 17:20.240
Why can't you bring her
to your place?

17:20.324 --> 17:21.646
I don't know, man.

17:21.714 --> 17:23.187
It's my aunt's
daughter. It's weird.

17:27.894 --> 17:29.905
Chris, honey,

17:29.972 --> 17:31.948
there's something
I want to talk to you about.

17:32.016 --> 17:33.423
Why, sure, Mom. Come on in.

17:33.491 --> 17:35.199
Chris, you know
your girlfriend, Lindsey?

17:35.267 --> 17:36.604
Yeah.

17:36.672 --> 17:38.682
Well, honey,

17:38.750 --> 17:41.397
I saw her making out
with another boy.

17:41.464 --> 17:43.575
Was she kissing with her mouth,
or her pants-mouth?

17:43.642 --> 17:44.881
Because she does both.

17:44.948 --> 17:47.123
Chris, I can't believe
you knew about this!

17:47.191 --> 17:48.733
That's terrible!

17:48.802 --> 17:51.080
Why would you even date
a girl like that?

17:51.147 --> 17:54.293
Well, because...
I-I thought she'd be like you.

17:54.361 --> 17:55.367
Like me?

17:55.434 --> 17:56.840
Well, you got to admit, Mom,

17:56.909 --> 17:59.085
she does look a lot like you.

17:59.154 --> 18:01.899
I mean, I just look at
you and Dad, you know?

18:01.951 --> 18:04.629
He's a complete train wreck,
and you put up with him.

18:04.713 --> 18:06.756
And I'm no prize myself.

18:06.807 --> 18:10.443
So, I guess I figured if I don't
find a girl just like you,

18:10.494 --> 18:12.417
I might never find anyone.

18:12.488 --> 18:14.179
Oh, Chris.

18:14.247 --> 18:16.086
Of course you'll find someone.

18:16.154 --> 18:17.529
<i>And you are a prize.</i>

18:17.596 --> 18:19.772
You're a handsome,
kind-hearted, young man,

18:19.840 --> 18:22.722
and any girl would be lucky
to have you in her life.

18:22.789 --> 18:24.060
You really think so?

18:24.128 --> 18:25.870
Of course I do, sweetheart.

18:25.938 --> 18:27.780
Thanks, Mo

18:29.323 --> 18:31.666
Now I'm gonna take the child
filter off the computer,

18:31.735 --> 18:33.776
and I want you
to have a fun night.

18:38.367 --> 18:39.672
Okay, this is it, James.

18:39.741 --> 18:41.516
I got your next
project right here.

18:41.584 --> 18:44.348
Peter, you've pitched me
dozens of projects,

18:44.415 --> 18:47.362
and all of them involve me
playing a hayride driver.

18:47.428 --> 18:49.706
I don't know, I just
see you doing that.

18:49.775 --> 18:51.969
You know, this year, it's
vampires, next year, it's hay.

18:52.036 --> 18:53.243
These things go in cycles.

18:53.310 --> 18:54.877
Peter, this is probably
a good time to fire you.

18:54.878 --> 18:56.151
What?!

18:56.176 --> 18:57.784
Come on, Peter,
who are we kidding?

18:57.866 --> 18:59.640
You're a one-man operation.

18:59.707 --> 19:01.415
You know me.
I need a whole crew.

19:01.499 --> 19:04.313
I need a drugs guy,
a girls guy, a fitness guy,

19:04.382 --> 19:07.867
a guy with a boat who can dump
stuff way-out-there kind of guy.

19:07.935 --> 19:09.373
But... But you're
my only client!

19:09.440 --> 19:10.579
Without you, I'm ruined!

19:10.647 --> 19:12.120
Well, that's show business.

19:12.187 --> 19:15.217
Now, if you'll excuse me, I have
to answer this fake phone call.

19:15.285 --> 19:16.761
Hello? Grimace
from McDonald's?

19:16.829 --> 19:18.535
Sorry, Peter,
I got to take this.

19:18.603 --> 19:19.942
No, I understand.

19:23.194 --> 19:24.767
(knocking)

19:26.609 --> 19:27.881
Hey, Tom.

19:27.948 --> 19:29.658
How you doing?

19:29.726 --> 19:30.898
<i>What do you want?</i>

19:30.966 --> 19:32.471
James Woods fired me.

19:32.539 --> 19:34.784
Listen, I-I been thinking.

19:34.852 --> 19:36.862
I was a real jerk to you.

19:36.930 --> 19:38.401
I dragged you out here,

19:38.470 --> 19:40.948
only to dump you
when you needed me the most.

19:41.017 --> 19:42.273
I guess what I'm saying is,

19:42.340 --> 19:44.517
I'm really sorry
about everything.

19:44.585 --> 19:46.125
Sorry doesn't
cut it, Peter.

19:46.193 --> 19:48.704
I quit my job, I left everything
behind to come out here.

19:48.772 --> 19:50.512
I know, but I was
just trying to help.

19:50.580 --> 19:52.088
I never wanted to let you down.

19:52.156 --> 19:56.008
Well, I suppose your only crime
is that you believed in me.

19:56.076 --> 19:58.354
And then stopped believing
in me rather abruptly.

19:58.421 --> 19:59.759
So what do you say?

19:59.827 --> 20:01.869
You ready to go back
to where we both belong?

20:01.937 --> 20:04.518
Yup. Let's go home.

20:04.586 --> 20:05.925
Sounds good.

20:05.992 --> 20:08.972
Hey, check out this picture
James Woods texted me.

20:09.040 --> 20:11.652
What is that? A balloon
running out of air?

20:11.720 --> 20:13.597
Yeah, see, I was hoping
you would know.

20:13.664 --> 20:14.602
(snaps fingers)

20:14.669 --> 20:16.243
It's his cousin's anus.

20:20.531 --> 20:22.004
Good evening, Quahog.
I'm Tom Tucker.

20:22.071 --> 20:23.546
And I'm Joyce Kinney.

20:23.614 --> 20:26.863
Tom, I think I speak for all of
us here at Channel Five News

20:26.930 --> 20:29.207
when I say it's good
to have you back here

20:29.276 --> 20:30.313
at half your
original salary.

20:30.381 --> 20:31.521
Still twice yours, Joyce.

20:31.588 --> 20:32.926
Our top story tonight:

20:32.994 --> 20:35.405
Quahog's first organic
supermarket opens this weekend.

20:35.473 --> 20:37.617
So head on down
before the gays turn it

20:37.686 --> 20:40.263
into a cruising ground like
they did the Barnes and Noble

20:40.331 --> 20:42.408
upstairs coffee bar,
which they had to close.

20:42.476 --> 20:44.787
Well, I'm just happy
Tom got his job back.

20:44.855 --> 20:47.130
But I'm sorry I missed meeting
your girlfriend, Chris.

20:47.197 --> 20:48.608
That's okay, Dad.

20:48.675 --> 20:50.046
Yeah, Chris, I
never asked you.

20:50.114 --> 20:52.360
What did Lindsey say when
you broke up with her?

20:52.427 --> 20:54.100
Oh, she didn't seem to mind.

20:54.168 --> 20:56.074
I think she already moved on
to someone else.

20:56.145 --> 20:57.786
Thanks for buying me
these clothes, Glenn.

20:57.854 --> 20:59.092
You're welcome.

20:59.160 --> 21:00.432
And you're dismissed.

21:00.500 --> 21:02.373
Do I still get
to keep the 20?

21:02.374 --> 22:03.374
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru